        <p>A straight-time year pays $8,382.40 of Social Security and sits $49,300.00 under the base. Five overtime hours a week close about half that gap, to $23,950.00 under. Ten overtime hours a week cross it: the year reaches $185,900.00 of gross, which is $1,400.00 over the base, so the last $1,400.00 pays no Social Security and saves $86.80. The crossing point is about 9.7 overtime hours a week, because $49,300.00 divided by $5,070.00, the gross one overtime hour a week adds in a year, is 9.72. Two things to keep straight. First, only Social Security stops. Medicare has no cap, so it keeps charging 1.45% on every dollar, $2,695.55 on the ten-overtime-hour year. Second, the saving is small on purpose: $86.80 on a $185,900.00 year is a rounding error, not a strategy. The base matters here for a different reason. It is the first rung on this ladder where overtime can push a year past it at all, which is one more way $65 behaves differently from every rate below it. Our <a href="/articles/what-is-fica-tax/">FICA guide</a> breaks the 7.65% into its two parts and the wage base, and the <a href="/overtime-calculator/">overtime calculator</a> prices any hours pattern you enter.</p>
