        <p>Thirteen unpaid weeks leave $80,164.90 of take-home, which beats a $100,000 salary by $984.90. At $64, the same thirteen weeks trailed it by $112.56, so the lead has swung by $1,097.46 in one rung. That makes $65 the first rate on this ladder where missing a full quarter of the year still clears six figures, and the reason is simple: thirteen unpaid weeks leave the same 1,560 paid hours as a 30-hour week worked all year, and a 30-hour week at $65 pays $101,400.00 gross. The table also hides two exact landings worth knowing. Two unpaid weeks land exactly on a $130,000 salary’s take-home, $100,121.00, to the cent. Four unpaid weeks land exactly on a full-time $60-an-hour year, $96,566.80, to the cent. So the quarter-year test is not just a curiosity. It is the fastest way to see where $65 sits: one rung above the line where a missed quarter costs you six figures, and two rungs above the line where it costs you the $130,000 comparison.</p>
