        <p>That take-home number comes with limits, and they matter. It is federal tax only, for a single filer taking the $16,100 standard deduction. No state tax is in it. No 401(k) and no health premium comes off it. It also assumes 40 paid hours in all 52 weeks, which no real year quite delivers. The detail most guides skip is in Step 2: $13,400 of the $119,100 taxable income actually sits in the 24% bracket, about 18% bigger than the $11,320 slice at $64, and that slice costs $3,216.00. Tax the whole year at a flat 22% and you overstate the federal bill by $5,020.00. The bracket-slice section below prices exactly what that slice means for your next raise. The sections that follow run the same $65.00 rate through every schedule, overtime, and unpaid-week variation, so you can read the number that matches your real year.</p>
