        <p>Read the last column from the top down and the pattern is the point of this section: the fewer hours you work, the more each hour keeps. At 20 hours a week you keep $54.22 an hour. At 40 hours you keep $49.84. The gap is $4.38 an hour on the same $65.00 rate, and the bracket is behind it. Only $1,100.00 of a 20-hour year’s $51,500.00 of taxable income reaches the 22% bracket. A 25-hour year carries $68,400.00, and $18,000.00 of it lands in the 22% bracket, so the 25-hour row is the first schedule on this page where the 22% bracket shows up in any real size. Three rows matter more than the headline. First, the 35-hour week crosses $100,000 gross on its own, at $118,300.00, and its $92,054.05 of take-home already beats a $115,000 salary by $2,321.55, before overtime or a raise. Second, the 37.5-hour paid week, common in offices that pay for a 7.5-hour day, is $126,750.00 gross, which still beats a $120,000 salary by $6,750.00 gross and $4,649.62 of take-home. At $64 the same week beat the $120,000 salary by only $3,316.80, so the lead has grown by $1,332.82 in one rung. A 2.5-hour shorter week costs $8,450.00 gross and $5,775.58 of take-home. Third, the step from 30 to 40 hours is worth $33,800.00 gross and $23,510.30 of take-home ($1,959.19 a month). That is the single biggest lever on this page, bigger than any raise in the nearby-rates table. Two more results are worth knowing. A 25-hour week at $65 beats a $80,000 salary’s take-home by $3,165.75, so even a part-time schedule at this rate out-earns a solid full-time salary. Part-time keeps a bigger share: at 20 hours you keep 83.4% of gross, against 76.7% full-time, and a 25-hour week at $65 beats a $75,000 salary’s take-home by $6,683.25. Our <a href="/articles/how-to-convert-part-time-hourly-pay-to-annual-salary/">part-time conversion guide</a> works the same ladder for any rate and any hours, and our <a href="/articles/how-many-hours-is-full-time/">full-time-hours guide</a> explains why 30 hours is the line where benefits usually start.</p>
