        <p>Two rows cause the confusion. Biweekly and semi-monthly are not the same check. Biweekly is 26 paychecks of $5,200.00. Semi-monthly is 24 paychecks of $5,666.67. The two extra biweekly checks a year are what make the totals equal. A month with three biweekly checks pays $15,600.00 gross ($11,962.53 take-home). A normal two-check month pays $10,400.00 ($7,975.02 take-home). That is why biweekly pay feels lumpy. The monthly average has its own trap: it divides by 12, not by 4. Four weeks is only $10,400.00, and the $866.67 difference is the extra 1.33 weeks the average month actually holds. Our <a href="/articles/how-many-paychecks-in-a-year/">paychecks-per-year guide</a> maps the three-paycheck months for every 2026 cycle, and our <a href="/articles/biweekly-vs-semi-monthly-pay-difference/">biweekly-versus-semimonthly comparison</a> runs the two schedules through a full year on the same salary.</p>
